+# Negative-claim discipline: scoped counterexamples, not phase failure
+
+This is a written N1-N8 scope review, not a formal N-packet PASS. The formal
+schema's minimum independent route count has not been supplied. That is a
+submission limitation, not evidence against the explicit counterexample.
+No exhausted-route or model-wide no-go conclusion is offered.
+
+## N1: Actual alternatives considered
+
+The target negative implication has one constructive refutation: the stated
+sparse ergodic current law. Several plausible attempts to repair that
+implication are addressed within that construction: exclude nonergodic
+global mixtures; impose cubic/reflection symmetry; require bounded diagonal
+covariance; require deterministically bounded enlarged filling supports;
+and use deterministic smooth sources. All those strengthened geometric
+premises still hold in the example. These are checks of its scope, not five
+distinct mathematical proof families, and are not counted as such.
+
+An actual Gibbs-weight premise is a different route and is NOT ruled out.
+The score note explicitly uses positive electric weights and local shifts
+to obtain a positive result. Direct susceptibility, a normalized polymer
+expansion, and a multiscale smooth-field treatment also remain available.
+No retained authority is cited as having closed those routes.
+
+## N2: Do not inflate the open conditions
+
+| Pair | Known relation |
+|---|---|
+| Full normalized compact source law / control of its diffuse filling correction | A suitable full source comparison could control the correction; no independence claimed |
+| Restricted static sine-score limit / physical principal-flux limit | The former alone does not identify the latter; converse under an appropriate source map is not examined |
+| Physical source limit / photon pole | A field limit with sufficient time/spectral information could imply a pole; the restricted thermal Ward result lacks that information |
+| Ergodic state premise / deterministic contact concentration | The stated ergodic application proves concentration, so these are not counted as separate walls |
+
+There is one central campaign target: the actual physical compact field and
+its state. The table records dependencies and unknown relations, not an
+asserted count of independent obstructions.
+
+## N3: Hidden-premise scan
+
+The rotor Hamiltonian, full thermal trace, sine observable and source class
+are supplied explicitly. Positive electric-walk weights are proved from
+that Hamiltonian, not attributed to the minimal framework axioms. The
+ergodic Gibbs state is a stated premise, not silently selected. The general
+source bound for finite-volume limits is distinguished from the weaker
+shift bound applicable to a chosen Gibbs component. Standard kernels,
+Feynman-Kac, the mean ergodic theorem and elementary central limit tools are
+identified as mathematical machinery. No physical coupling is fitted.
+
+## N4: Exact residual matching
+
+The only negative witness for sparse geometry is the construction in this
+packet, for diffuse LOCAL FILLING coefficients. It is not a witness about
+the Hamiltonian's principal flux or a constant-edge current test, where
+conservation would cancel. The free circle addresses inheritance of
+derivatives through a winding mixture. The independent rotor addresses
+the insufficiency of a restricted Ward-sector Gaussian test for a photon.
+None is substituted for another residual or for an axiom contradiction.
+
+## N5: Resolution of the rhetoric
+
+The sparse-current statement is infinite-volume and includes deterministic
+smooth diffuse tests. The formula challenges are finite instances only.
+The compact cover identities are finite-graph identities. The actual-law
+Gaussian statement is restricted to finite-beta, static exact curls with
+contact concentration; general space-time, ground-state or principal-flux
+claims are not made. "Not a photon theorem" describes the absent physical
+identification, not a proof that the model lacks photons.
+
+## N6: Partial positive paths
+
+The actual positive weights already repair one missing control: the sine
+MGF is sub-Gaussian. Static shifts plus an explicit concentration premise
+give a limited Gaussian result. A stronger source/state proof could extend
+that progress without any new axiom. The approved isotropy primitive is
+not asserted to select a Hamiltonian, a coupling, or a phase.
+
+## N7: Steelman
+
+The strongest positive objection is that a local Hamiltonian law excludes
+the sparse construction's coordinated product-sign environment. That is
+accepted: the counterexample targets geometry-only inference, while the
+actual-law calculation uses precisely the additional weight structure.
+A second objection is that a physically useful source could avoid the
+uncontrolled filling observable. That route remains open and motivates
+the dynamic-response work. No premise is changed to defeat it.
+
+## N8: Cross-cycle echo
+
+Earlier main examples used global mixtures to show that covariance/sparse
+supports do not imply a Gaussian law. The new construction keeps ergodicity,
+full cubic symmetry and diagonal bounded covariance, with an exact
+non-Gaussian diffuse limit. The cover identity itself is known machinery.
+The new positive content is the actual compact score bound and its
+conditional Gaussian sector, not another rephrasing of a missing phase.
+Repeated Ward examples would add no new physical premise and are not the
+next campaign target.
